(<a href="#A2.E13" title="Equation B.13 ‣ Lemma B.1 (Uniform macroscopic coercivity). ‣ B.2 Estimates for the reflection and half-turn operators ‣ Appendix B Operator formulation and modified 𝐿^2 contraction ‣ Accelerated High-Accuracy Sampling from a Warm Startvia the Proximal Bouncy Particle Sampler" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.13</span></a>). It suffices to consider
<math id="A3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in C_{\rm c}^{\infty}(\mathbb{R}^{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>c</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in C_{\rm c}^{\infty}(\mathbb{R}^{d})</annotation></semantics></math>, the general case following by density and
closedness of the gradient.</span></p>
